  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some lingering showers this morning before\nturning cooler with less humidity on Monday and Tuesday.\n\nEarly morning local radar imagery shows multiple areas of light\nto moderate shower activity. A bulk of this is emerging off the\nAllegheny Front and tracking across the Shenandoah Valley. \nDespite the somewhat lackluster appearance of these showers, \nquasi-tropical rain rates are being observed. Based on upstream\ntrends, 3-hour precipitation totals are generally around 0.25 \nto 0.50 inches, locally approaching an inch in spots. Despite \nthe 06Z RAP objective analysis depicting most unstable CAPE \nvalues in the 500-750 J/kg range, this has not been enough to \nproduce any recent lightning activity. The lone exception is \napproaching Highland County from the south as storms lift away \nfrom Hot Springs, Virginia. With most hydrographs featuring \ncurrent water levels well below any pre-defined stages, any \nflooding risk appears low at this time.\n\nThe array of showers will progress eastward to the I-95 corridor\nby 4-6 AM timeframe before exiting off into northeastern\nMaryland by just after sunrise. While much of the remainder of\nthe day will be dry, a warm front just south of the Richmond\nmetro area is forecast to cross the region later this morning.\nThis may help induce a few additional showers, but these should\nbe rather disorganized in nature. By the afternoon, expect more\nsun than clouds which will help crank temperatures back well \ninto the 80s. This is aided by a weak downslope component to the\nwinds as they shift to northwesterly. Despite the wind shift, \nhumidity levels do remain elevated with dew points in the upper \n60s to low 70s.\n\nHeading into tonight, an approaching cold front from the Great\nLakes may induce additional showers along and west of the\nAllegheny Front. Otherwise, this is largely a drier frontal\npassage which will also lead to cool temperatures and lower\nhumidity to start off the work week. Gradients are rather tight\nfor mid-September as a broad 1024-mb surface high approaches\nfrom the Great Lakes. This will favor a breezy northwesterly\nwind for Monday, with afternoon gusts up to 20 to 25 mph at\ntimes. Forecast highs are in the mid 70s to low 80s (5 to 15\ndegrees cooler for mountain locales). As the seasonably strong\ndome of high pressure settles over the northeastern U.S. on\nMonday night, low temperatures plunge into the upper 40s to 50s.\nThe autumnal feel to the air lingers into Tuesday before an\neventual warm up by mid-week. Plenty of sunshine can be expected\neach day, but with decreasing winds on Tuesday with the ridge\nalong the Eastern Seaboard. This all comes with dew points in\nthe upper 40s to mid 50s which is certainly a change from recent\ndays.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warm and dry conditions through the end of the\nweek; showers possible along a cold front next weekend.\n\nA strong upper ridge builds over the region during the middle \nportion of the week supporting very warm and dry conditions. Not\nlooking at any record highs since records are in the mid 90s. \nThe ridge begins to flatten at the end of the week as it starts \nfeeling pressure from a deepening trough over Hudson Bay. A \nfront eventually sinks southward at the end of the week possibly\nbringing few-sct showers. Overall, very warm and dry this week,\nturning cooler and potentially wetter for late September.",
